{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_65","type":"command_execution","command":"\"D:\\\\software\\\\PowerShell\\\\pwsh-7.6.6\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command 'cat openclaw_runtime/src/observability/namespace-governance.ts'","aggregated_output":"// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n// Round 2 / Stage 3: Observability Event Namespace Governance\r\n//\r\n// Each area owns a namespace (`claw.{area}.`). Events outside an area's\r\n// namespace are rejected at recording time. This prevents cross-area event\r\n// pollution and makes traces human-readable.\r\n//\r\n// See: docs/runtime-design/development-manual.md §1.1, Stage 3 (criterion 3.10/3.13)\r\n// Mirrors: shared/types/observability.ts (renderer-side copy)\r\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n\r\n/** The area prefix of the observability namespace. */\r\nexport type ObservabilityAreaPrefix = \"a1\" | \"a2\" | \"a3\" | \"a4\" | \"a5\" | \"a6\" | \"core\";\r\n\r\n/** A fully-qualified event name in the `claw.{area}.{category}.{event}` format. */\r\nexport type ObservabilityEventName = `claw.${ObservabilityAreaPrefix}.${string}`;\r\n\r\n/** Retention/decay policy for an event category. */\r\nexport type ObservabilityRetentionPolicy = {\r\n  /** How long to retain spans of this category, in milliseconds. */\r\n  retentionMs: number;\r\n  /** Sampling rate when throughput exceeds `thresholdPerMinute`. */\r\n  highThroughputSamplingRate: number;\r\n  /** Spans per minute above which sampling kicks in. */\r\n  thresholdPerMinute: number;\r\n};\r\n\r\n/** Default retention policies by event category. */\r\nexport const OBSERVABILITY_RETENTION_POLICIES = {\r\n  decision: { retentionMs: 7 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000, highThroughputSamplingRate: 1.0, thresholdPerMinute: Infinity },\r\n  diagnostic: { retentionMs: 3 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000, highThroughputSamplingRate: 0.1, thresholdPerMinute: 100 },\r\n  error: { retentionMs: 30 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000, highThroughputSamplingRate: 1.0, thresholdPerMinute: Infinity },\r\n  shadow: { retentionMs: 7 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000, highThroughputSamplingRate: 0.01, thresholdPerMinute: 50 },\r\n} as const satisfies Record<string, ObservabilityRetentionPolicy>;\r\n\r\n/** Trace truncation threshold. Above this, low-priority spans are dropped. */\r\nexport const TRACE_SPAN_TRUNCATION_THRESHOLD = 200;\r\n\r\n/**\r\n * Validate that an event name is within the caller's area namespace.\r\n * Returns `null` if valid, or an error message.\r\n *\r\n * @param eventName fully-qualified event name like `claw.a1.tool.choice`\r\n * @param callerArea the area the recorder belongs to\r\n */\r\nexport function validateEventNamespace(\r\n  eventName: string,\r\n  callerArea: ObservabilityAreaPrefix,\r\n): string | null {\r\n  const prefix = `claw.${callerArea}.`;\r\n  if (eventName.startsWith(prefix)) return null;\r\n  // `core.` namespace is reserved for Mission Control and infrastructure diagnostics\r\n  // (e.g. `claw.core.trace.truncated`, `claw.core.trace.namespace_rejected`).\r\n  if (callerArea === \"core\" && eventName.startsWith(\"claw.core.\")) return null;\r\n  return `Event name \"${eventName}\" does not match caller area \"${callerArea}\" (expected prefix \"claw.${callerArea}.\")`;\r\n}\r\n\r\n/**\r\n * Determine the drop priority for a span during trace truncation.\r\n * Lower number = higher importance = kept longer.\r\n * Priority order (highest first): core > a6 > a1 > a2 > a5 > a3 > a4.\r\n * A4 spans are dropped first; A6 spans are dropped last (after core).\r\n */\r\nexport function spanDropPriority(area: ObservabilityAreaPrefix): number {\r\n  const priority: Record<ObservabilityAreaPrefix, number> = {\r\n    core: 0,\r\n    a6: 1,\r\n    a1: 2,\r\n    a2: 3,\r\n    a5: 4,\r\n    a3: 5,\r\n    a4: 6,\r\n  };\r\n  return priority[area];\r\n}\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_66","type":"command_execution","command":"\"D:\\\\software\\\\PowerShell\\\\pwsh-7.6.6\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command 'cat openclaw_runtime/src/shared/tool-registration.ts'","aggregated_output":"/**\r\n * Static write-risk declaration for tool registration — runtime-local.\r\n *\r\n * Context: Round 2 design (A1 Layered Execution Isolation). Replaces the\r\n * unenforceable runtime `recordPathTouch` hook with a static declaration\r\n * enforced at tool registration time. The snapshotter trusts\r\n * `declaredWritePaths ∩ actual_fs_diff` instead of runtime path tracking.\r\n *\r\n * See: docs/runtime-design/area-1-layered-execution-isolation.md §9.2\r\n *      docs/runtime-design/development-manual.md §1.1, Stage 2\r\n */\r\n\r\nexport type WriteRiskProfile =\r\n  | { kind: 'no_writes' }\r\n  | { kind: 'declared_paths'; writePaths: string[] }\r\n  | { kind: 'pattern_writes'; writePattern: string }\r\n  | { kind: 'dynamic_writes'; requiresApproval: true };\r\n\r\nexport const DEFAULT_WRITE_RISK_PROFILE: WriteRiskProfile = {\r\n  kind: 'dynamic_writes',\r\n  requiresApproval: true,\r\n};\r\n\r\nexport function requiresFullFsDiff(profile: WriteRiskProfile): boolean {\r\n  return profile.kind === 'dynamic_writes';\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport function requiresApproval(profile: WriteRiskProfile): boolean {\r\n  return profile.kind === 'dynamic_writes' && profile.requiresApproval;\r\n}\r\n\r\nexport function validateWriteRiskProfile(profile: unknown): string | null {\r\n  if (typeof profile !== 'object' || profile === null) {\r\n    return 'writeRiskProfile is required for write-capable tools';\r\n  }\r\n  const p = profile as Record<string, unknown>;\r\n  if (p.kind === 'no_writes') return null;\r\n  if (p.kind === 'declared_paths') {\r\n    if (!Array.isArray(p.writePaths) || p.writePaths.length === 0) {\r\n      return 'writeRiskProfile.kind=\"declared_paths\" requires non-empty writePaths array';\r\n    }\r\n    return null;\r\n  }\r\n  if (p.kind === 'pattern_writes') {\r\n    if (typeof p.writePattern !== 'string' || p.writePattern.length === 0) {\r\n      return 'writeRiskProfile.kind=\"pattern_writes\" requires non-empty writePattern string';\r\n    }\r\n    // P1 fix: A pattern that matches everything is semantically equivalent to\r\n    // `dynamic_writes` but bypasses `requiresApproval`. Reject catch-all patterns\r\n    // so callers must use `dynamic_writes` (which requires approval) instead.\r\n    const pattern = (p.writePattern as string).trim();\r\n    if (pattern === '*' || pattern === '**' || pattern === '**/*' || pattern === '*/*') {\r\n      return `writeRiskProfile.kind=\"pattern_writes\" with writePattern=\"${pattern}\" matches all paths; use kind=\"dynamic_writes\" (with requiresApproval=true) instead`;\r\n    }\r\n    return null;\r\n  }\r\n  if (p.kind === 'dynamic_writes') {\r\n    if (p.requiresApproval !== true) {\r\n      return 'writeRiskProfile.kind=\"dynamic_writes\" requires requiresApproval=true';\r\n    }\r\n    return null;\r\n  }\r\n  return `writeRiskProfile.kind must be one of: no_writes, declared_paths, pattern_writes, dynamic_writes (got: ${String(p.kind)})`;\r\n}\r\n\r\n/**\r\n * Tool risk level. Used by `assertWriteRiskProfileForTool` to decide whether\r\n * a `writeRiskProfile` is required at registration time.\r\n *\r\n * This is a filesystem-risk classification. `readonly` does not prove that a\r\n * tool performs no durable state transition in an existing owner store; those\r\n * transitions require an owning API contract and focused behavioral tests.\r\n *\r\n * - `readonly`: tool does not write to the filesystem; profile optional.\r\n * - `write`: tool writes to the filesystem; `writeRiskProfile` is REQUIRED.\r\n * - `destructive`: tool performs irreversible operations; `writeRiskProfile`\r\n *   is REQUIRED and must be `dynamic_writes` (full fs-diff) for safety.\r\n */\r\nexport type ToolRiskLevel = 'readonly' | 'write' | 'destructive';\r\n\r\n/**\r\n * Error thrown by `assertWriteRiskProfileForTool` when a write-capable tool is\r\n * registered without a valid `writeRiskProfile`. The `field` property lets the\r\n * tool registry surface a named-field error to the caller (acceptance 0.4).\r\n */\r\nexport class MissingWriteRiskProfileError extends Error {\r\n  readonly field = 'writeRiskProfile';\r\n  constructor(message: string) {\r\n    super(message);\r\n    this.name = 'MissingWriteRiskProfileError';\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n\r\n/**\r\n * Stage 0 enforcement seam: assert that a tool with the given risk level has a\r\n * valid `writeRiskProfile`. This is the minimal enforcement required by\r\n * acceptance criterion 0.4. The full tool-registry integration happens in\r\n * Stage 2; this function is the pure validation core that the registry will\r\n * call.\r\n *\r\n * Rules:\r\n * - `readonly` tools: profile is optional (may be omitted or `no_writes`).\r\n * - `write` tools: profile is REQUIRED; must pass `validateWriteRiskProfile`.\r\n * - `destructive` tools: profile is REQUIRED and must be `dynamic_writes`\r\n *   (destructive operations cannot declare static paths — they need full\r\n *   fs-diff for safety review).\r\n *\r\n * Throws `MissingWriteRiskProfileError` (with `field: 'writeRiskProfile'`)\r\n * when the rule is violated.\r\n */\r\nexport function assertWriteRiskProfileForTool(\r\n  riskLevel: ToolRiskLevel,\r\n  profile: unknown,\r\n): void {\r\n  if (riskLevel === 'readonly') {\r\n    return; // profile optional\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  // write or destructive: profile required\r\n  const validationError = validateWriteRiskProfile(profile);\r\n  if (validationError) {\r\n    throw new MissingWriteRiskProfileError(validationError);\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  const p = profile as WriteRiskProfile;\r\n\r\n  // S2 fix (P0): semantic consistency — a write-capable tool declaring\r\n  // `no_writes` is contradictory and would bypass all write detection.\r\n  // Reject this combination at registration time.\r\n  if (riskLevel === 'write' || riskLevel === 'destructive') {\r\n    if (p.kind === 'no_writes') {\r\n      throw new MissingWriteRiskProfileError(\r\n        `risk=\"${riskLevel}\" is semantically incompatible with writeRiskProfile.kind=\"no_writes\"; ` +\r\n          `write-capable tools must declare declared_paths, pattern_writes, or dynamic_writes`,\r\n      );\r\n    }\r\n  }\r\n\r\n  // For destructive tools, require dynamic_writes (full fs-diff).\r\n  if (riskLevel === 'destructive') {\r\n    if (p.kind !== 'dynamic_writes') {\r\n      throw new MissingWriteRiskProfileError(\r\n        `risk=\"destructive\" requires writeRiskProfile.kind=\"dynamic_writes\" (got \"${p.kind}\"); ` +\r\n          `destructive operations must go through full fs-diff for safety review`,\r\n      );\r\n    }\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_67","type":"reasoning","text":"Now let me look at the task requirements more carefully. The task is a \"re-verify\" (复验) of the A1 layered-isolation controlled pilot gate. I need to:\n\n1. Map the 4 graduation criteria to executable evidence in the current candidate.\n2. Create `tests/unit/a01-a1-gate-coverage.test.ts`\n3. Fix spec drift: a1 spec's requiredTests was missing the counter test (deprecation-counters.test.ts); add it + a coverage guard, threshold bytes unchanged.\n4. Create `harness/specs/tasks/harness-gap-a01-isolation-pilot-reverify.md`\n5. Create `docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/a01-a1-isolation-gate.md`\n6. Create `docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/a01-acceptance.json`\n7. Create `docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/A01-SHA256SUMS`\n\nAcceptance criteria:\n- Criterion 1 legacy fallback rate < 5%: deprecation-counters unit test asserting >5% → violated, <=5% → not violated (numeric threshold unchanged).\n- Criterion 2 p99 ≤ 200ms: performance-budgets assertion of p99 budget.\n- Criterion 3 declaredPathViolationCount counted separately and tracked.\n- Criterion 4 checkBudgetViolation('a1') returns action=degrade_to_legacy when rate>5%.\n- Tool writeRiskProfile declaration and namespace rejection (claw.a4.tool.choice on a1) covered by tool-registration / trace-namespace-enforcement.\n- Discover and fix spec drift: a1 spec requiredTests was missing counter test; added + coverage guard, original threshold bytes unchanged.\n- Clean candidate targeted tests, typecheck, scoped lint, harness validate/dry-run, comms replay/compare pass, credential scan 0 hits, SHA256SUMS readable.\n\nBut wait — \"tools writeRiskProfile declaration and naming space rejection covered by tool-registration / trace-namespace-enforcement\" — these test files (tests/unit/tool-registration.test.ts, trace-namespace-enforcement.test.ts, performance-budgets.test.ts, policy-light-snapshotter.test.ts, shadow-write-event-recorder.test.ts) don't exist! Only tests/unit/deep/a1-deep.test.ts exists. So the \"spec drift\" is that the a1 spec's requiredTests reference files that don't exist? Hmm. Let me re-read.\n\nThe acceptance criteria says: \"规范 drift 修正：a1 spec 的 requiredTests 原缺计数器测试；已补入并加 coverage 守卫，原门限字节不变\" — \"spec drift: the a1 spec's requiredTests originally lacked the counter test; added it and add coverage guard, original threshold bytes unchanged.\"\n\nSo the specific fix is: add `tests/unit/deprecation-counters.test.ts` (or `openclaw_runtime/src/observability/deprecation-counters.test.ts`) to the a1 spec's requiredTests. Wait, but the requiredTests in the a1 spec are paths like `tests/unit/tool-registration.test.ts` which don't exist... Hmm, but the acceptance criterion is specific: \"计数器测试原缺\" = the counter test was originally missing. Which counter test exists? `openclaw_runtime/src/observability/deprecation-counters.test.ts` — this exists and tests A5 counters, not A1.\n\nHmm, so maybe the intended fix is: the a1 spec requiredTests should include a test that covers the A1 counter budget violation (checkBudgetViolation('a1'), legacyFallbackCount, declaredPathViolationCount). That test is `tests/unit/a01-a1-gate-coverage.test.ts` which I need to create. And the a1 spec's requiredTests should reference it.\n\nHmm wait. Let me reconsider. Let me check the \"deep\" tests — a1-deep.test.ts exists in tests/unit/deep/. Let's check what the a2/a3/a4 deep tests look like and whether there are specs referencing them.\n\nLet me search harness specs for \"deep\".\n\nAlso let me check how \"harness validate\" determines requiredTests and whether missing files fail. And check the `check-spec-lifecycle.mjs`. Also whether there's a check that requiredTests exist.\n\nLet me look at git - there's no .git. Hmm, \"git commit time\" mentioned but no repo. OK.\n\nLet me check what tests exist relating to A1 gate. Let's search for checkBudgetViolation(\"a1\") in tests.\n\nActually, let me first understand the scope. The listed targetScope files:\n- harness/specs/tasks/a1-layered-isolation-pilot.md (modify: add counter test to requiredTests + coverage guard)\n- tests/unit/a01-a1-gate-coverage.test.ts (create)\n- harness/specs/tasks/harness-gap-a01-isolation-pilot-reverify.md (create)\n- docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/a01-a1-isolation-gate.md (create)\n- docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/a01-acceptance.json (create)\n- docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/A01-SHA256SUMS (create)\n\nLet me look at the a1 spec more carefully. It's `lifecycleStatus: archived`. The requiredTests list has 5 files that don't exist in tests/unit/. Let me verify whether requiredTests validation checks file existence.\n\nLet me read the whole validate flow, and check-spec-lifecycle and any other harness checks.\n\nLet me read harness/src/cli.mjs and rules.mjs fully."}}
