s\\apply-patch.ts              122\r\nD:\\tmp\\rsi-tf0604-run5\\trials\\tf-0293-a01-treatment-3\\agent\\openclaw_runtime\\src\\agents\\bash-tools.exec.ts         1501\r\nD:\\tmp\\rsi-tf0604-run5\\trials\\tf-0293-a01-treatment-3\\agent\\openclaw_runtime\\src\\agents\\bash-tools.exec.ts         1502\r\nD:\\tmp\\rsi-tf0604-run5\\trials\\tf-0293-a01-treatment-3\\agent\\openclaw_runtime\\src\\agents\\bash-tools.process.ts       256\r\nD:\\tmp\\rsi-tf0604-run5\\trials\\tf-0293-a01-treatment-3\\agent\\openclaw_runtime\\src\\agents\\bash-tools.process.ts       257\r\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_129","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I have a good understanding. The task: create a new coverage test `tests/unit/a01-a1-gate-coverage.test.ts` that maps the 4 graduation criteria to executable evidence, create the gap task spec `harness/specs/tasks/harness-gap-a01-isolation-pilot-reverify.md`, docs `docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/a01-a1-isolation-gate.md`, `a01-acceptance.json`, `A01-SHA256SUMS`, and fix the spec drift: add the missing counter test to the a1 spec's requiredTests and add a coverage guard, keeping thresholds byte-identical.\n\nLet me verify acceptance criteria mapping to executable evidence:\n1. Criterion 1: legacy fallback rate < 5% — deprecation-counters unit test asserts >5% → violated, <=5% → not violated (threshold unchanged).\n2. Criterion 2: p99 ≤ 200ms — performance-budgets assertion on p99 budget. But there's no performance-budgets test file. Hmm, I need to create executable evidence. Task says \"逐条把4个毕业判据映射到当前候选的可执行证据\" (map each of the 4 graduation criteria to executable evidence for the current candidate). Criterion 2 asserts p99 budget. Let me look at what exists for p99. There's `scripts/lib/runtime-perf-benchmark.mjs` with summarize producing p99. The budget is 200ms criterion 4.5.\n\nLet me check the a1 deep test and check what's in place. Actually, the acceptance criteria says \"判据2 p99 ≤ 200ms：performance-budgets 断言 p99 预算\" — i.e. the coverage test must assert the p99 budget. Since there's no `performance-budgets.test.ts`, but this new coverage test file `tests/unit/a01-a1-gate-coverage.test.ts` will host the assertion. Hmm, but the targetScope lists only these files:\n- harness/specs/tasks/a1-layered-isolation-pilot.md\n- tests/unit/a01-a1-gate-coverage.test.ts\n- harness/specs/tasks/harness-gap-a01-isolation-pilot-reverify.md\n- docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/a01-a1-isolation-gate.md\n- docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/a01-acceptance.json\n- docs/dev/harness-gap-closure/A01-SHA256SUMS\n\nSo the coverage test lives in `tests/unit/a01-a1-gate-coverage.test.ts` and is the main new test. The a1 spec's requiredTests originally missing the counter test — but actually all 5 requiredTests paths don't exist! Let me check: tool-registration.test.ts, policy-light-snapshotter.test.ts, shadow-write-event-recorder.test.ts, trace-namespace-enforcement.test.ts, performance-budgets.test.ts — none exist. So the \"spec drift\" is that a1 spec's requiredTests declares nonexistent paths. The acceptance says \"发现并修正 spec drift：a1 spec 的 requiredTests 原缺计数器测试；已补入并加 coverage 守卫，原门限字节不变\" — \"the a1 spec's requiredTests originally lacked the counter test; add it and add coverage guard, original thresholds byte unchanged\".\n\nSo specifically: the counter test (deprecation-counters) is missing from requiredTests. We add `openclaw_runtime/src/observability/deprecation-counters.test.ts` to requiredTests. And add a coverage guard (in the new test) that ensures the requiredTests resolve / that the gate criteria are covered.\n\nBut wait — the other requiredTests paths are also nonexistent. Hmm. That's a bigger drift. But the acceptance criterion is specific: \"requiredTests 原缺计数器测试\" — \"originally missing the counter test\". So the fix is to add the counter test to requiredTests. Perhaps also we should be careful about what's the true scope. Let me re-read the objective:\n\n\"A01 按原 spec 门限复验 A1 Layered Execution Isolation 的受控 pilot 门：逐条把 4 个毕业判据映射到当前候选的可执行证据；不重定义门限；记录 spec requiredTests 缺口修正（计数器测试原缺）与残余。\"\n\n\"Re-verify A1's controlled pilot gate per original spec thresholds: map each of the 4 graduation criteria to executable evidence for the current candidate; do not redefine thresholds; record the spec requiredTests gap fix (counter test originally missing) and residual.\"\n\nSo: the coverage test maps 4 criteria to executable evidence. The spec drift fix = add counter test to requiredTests (and coverage guard).\n\nNow the 4 criteria:\n1. legacy fallback rate < 5% (deprecation-counters: rate >5% → violated; <=5% → not violated)\n2. p99 ≤ 200ms (performance-budgets asserts p99 budget)\n3. declaredPathViolationCount separately counted and tracked\n4. checkBudgetViolation('a1') returns action=degrade_to_legacy when rate>5%\n\nPlus: writeRiskProfile declarations & namespace rejection covered by tool-registration / trace-namespace-enforcement.\n\nFor criterion 2 (p99), I need executable evidence. There's no existing p99 A1 budget test. The coverage test can assert:\n- `spanDropPriority`, or something... Actually let's find the isolation hot path. Hmm.\n\nLet me think about what \"performance-budgets 断言 p99 预算\" means as executable evidence. Perhaps the coverage test asserts the p99 budget constant from the perf benchmark. Let me check `scripts/lib/runtime-perf-benchmark.mjs` and `scripts/check-runtime-perf-report.mjs` to see if there's a budget.\n\nActually, since the task says the coverage test file is `tests/unit/a01-a1-gate-coverage.test.ts`, and criterion 2 is \"p99 ≤ 200ms：performance-budgets 断言 p99 预算\", maybe I create a real executable p99 assertion in the coverage test that drives an actual A1 hot-path operation (e.g., `beginSnapshot` with a1 enabled using copy-on-write, or a trace recorder) and measures p99 over N iterations, asserting ≤ 200ms. That would be genuine executable evidence for the current candidate.\n\nWait, but the coverage test lives in tests/unit, environment jsdom. beginSnapshot does filesystem/git work. Hmm. Let me consider a simpler, deterministic approach: measure p99 of the pure A1 helper functions (collectDeclaredWritePaths / detectUndeclaredWrites) over 100 iterations — mirroring \"100-turn session\" in criterion 2.9/4.5. That's genuine executable evidence of the A1 hot path budget.\n\nHmm, but is that \"the isolation hot path\"? The A1 deep test already has a 1000-path under 50ms stress test. For the coverage gate, I'll add a p99 budget assertion measured over 100 iterations of the A1 declared-path collection + shadow detection path, asserting p99 ≤ 200ms (criterion 4.5/2.9). And assert the budget constant 200 is unchanged.\n\nActually, safer: define a named constant `A1_P99_BUDGET_MS = 200` in the coverage test and assert measured p99 ≤ that. That is the \"performance-budgets 断言 p99 预算\".\n\nHmm, but the acceptance criterion explicitly names \"performance-budgets\" as the test file. There isn't one. Maybe I should reconsider: perhaps the intended fix IS to create/repair these test files? But targetScope doesn't include them. targetScope lists exactly 6 files. So the coverage test must be the single home for these mappings. But then \"工具 writeRiskProfile 声明与命名空间拒绝...由 tool-registration / trace-namespace-enforcement 覆盖\" — \"covered by tool-registration / trace-namespace-enforcement\" implies those tests exist elsewhere. They don't exist under those names, but the a1-deep test covers tool-registration, and observability-namespace.test.ts covers namespace. Hmm.\n\nGiven ambiguity, the safest interpretation consistent with targetScope: I create `tests/unit/a01-a1-gate-coverage.test.ts` that:\n- imports the real source modules (deprecation-counters, tool-registration, namespace-governance, shadow-write-event-recorder, policy-light-snapshotter, runtime-perf-benchmark summarize) and asserts each of the 4 criteria + the tool/namespace coverage claims.\n- Reads the a1 spec and asserts its `requiredTests` now includes the counter test + a coverage guard (every requiredTests entry resolves to an existing file, or is a known path). Actually \"加 coverage 守卫\" — add a coverage guard. The guard should ensure each of the 5 declared requiredTests files exists AND is referenced/covered. Hmm, but they don't exist.\n\nWait. Let me reconsider. Maybe the requiredTests drift is more than just the counter test, and the honest fix is: the requiredTests listed are stale/nonexistent legacy paths. The acceptance says specifically the *counter test was missing*. Let me verify: does the spec's requiredTests include anything for counters? No. So the counter criterion (criterion 1 & 4) currently has NO declared required test in the spec. That's the drift: the counter test is missing. We add it.\n\nThe other 4 declared paths are the pre-existing (legacy) declared tests. Since they don't exist in the repo, the coverage guard must *detect* that and report. Hmm, but then the guard would fail. Let me reconsider: maybe the coverage guard should assert that the *gate criteria* are covered by *executable evidence in the repo* (i.e., this new coverage test + a1-deep + observability-namespace), rather than assert the legacy declared paths exist.\n\nLet me re-read acceptance criterion: \"发现并修正 spec drift：a1 spec 的 requiredTests 原缺计数器测试；已补入并加 coverage 守卫，原门限字节不变。\" = \"Discover and fix spec drift: the a1 spec's requiredTests originally lacked the counter test; added it and added a coverage guard; original thresholds byte-unchanged.\"\n\nSo \"coverage guard\" = a guard that ensures the spec's requiredTests (and/or the gate criteria) are backed by executable evidence. The guard lives in the coverage test. Since some declared paths don't exist, maybe I should ALSO fix those? But targetScope doesn't allow the other test files... Actually targetScope is the scope of *changed* files. If I create tests/unit/tool-registration.test.ts etc., those would be outside targetScope, violating the ownership gate.\n\nHmm. Let me check whether the a1 spec's requiredTests paths might exist elsewhere (e.g., openclaw_runtime/src/... or a different dir). I searched and found no `tool-registration.test.ts`, `policy-light-snapshotter.test.ts`, `shadow-write-event-recorder.test.ts`, `trace-namespace-enforcement.test.ts`, `performance-budgets.test.ts`. So all 5 are dangling.\n\nHmm — actually wait. Maybe the claim in the task is that the a1 spec is `lifecycleStatus: archived` and these legacy test paths never existed; the drift is specifically that the *counter test* is missing from requiredTests. The coverage guard then maps criteria→evidence and asserts the requiredTests contain the counter test and that the gate's actual executable evidence exists.\n\nI think the cleanest honest solution:\n\n1. In `a1-layered-isolation-pilot.md`, add the counter test path to requiredTests **without touching the graduation-gate thresholds or acceptance text** (byte-identical thresholds). Add `openclaw_runtime/src/observability/deprecation-counters.test.ts`. Possibly also add my new coverage test path.\n\n2. Create `tests/unit/a01-a1-gate-coverage.test.ts` — the coverage guard: for each of the 4 graduation criteria, execute real assertions against the current candidate's source modules, and assert the a1 spec's requiredTests include the counter test and that each declared requiredTests entry either resolves on disk or is explicitly listed as a recorded residual.\n\nHmm, \"resolves on disk or recorded residual\" is a bit hand-wavy. Let me reconsider what a \"coverage guard\" should do concretely: it should fail if the spec's requiredTests drift (e.g., counter test removed) — i.e., assert the spec declares the counter test, and assert that the gate thresholds in the spec are the original ones (5%, 200ms, degrade_to_legacy). That is a guard against redefining thresholds. Good — \"不重定义门限\" (don't redefine thresholds) is verifiable by asserting the spec text contains the original threshold strings (0.05/5%/200ms) and that the source module uses those exact values. That's a solid guard.\n\nLet me now decide the exact content.\n\nCoverage test assertions:\n- Criterion 1: \n  - `deprecationCounters` A1: with legacyFallback=6, shadowSuccess=94 → rate 6% > 5% → violated true.\n  - With 5/95 → rate exactly 5% → not violated (strict >).\n  - Threshold is 0.05 (assert constant not redefined: assert the boundary behavior).\n- Criterion 2: p99 budget:\n  - Assert the A1 p99 budget is 200ms (a constant). Run N=100 iterations of the A1 snapshot-planning hot path (collectDeclaredWritePaths) and compute p99; assert ≤ 200.\n  - Also assert `summarize` p99 helper consistency? Better to import from runtime-perf-benchmark? That is a scripts .mjs. Can import via relative path from tests/unit: `../../scripts/lib/runtime-perf-benchmark.mjs`. Vitest handles .mjs (stripShebang plugin). Good — use its `summarize` to compute p99, tying the p99 definition to the repo's canonical percentile helper. That makes the p99 evidence authentic rather than ad-hoc.\n- Criterion 3: declaredPathViolationCount separately counted:\n  - `incrementA1DeclaredPathViolation(3)` → snapshot().a1.declaredPathViolationCount === 3, and it's independent from legacyFallbackCount/shadowSuccessCount.\n  - Also assert \"tracked\": that it's part of snapshot and not conflated.\n- Criterion 4: checkBudgetViolation('a1'):\n  - rate>5% → violated && action==='degrade_to_legacy'.\n  - rate<=5% → violated===false.\n- Tool writeRiskProfile declarations: assert the 6 built-in tools (write/edit/bash/apply_patch/exec/process) each declare writeRiskProfile + risk. Hmm, that requires reading the source files or their exported definitions. Do these tools carry a `risk`/`writeRiskProfile` field? Let me check the tool source files. Earlier I found `writeRiskProfile` string references in write.ts, edit.ts, bash.ts, apply-patch.ts, bash-tools.exec.ts, bash-tools.process.ts (2 lines each). Let me check those lines.\n\nLet me actually inspect those lines.\n\nAlso the acceptance for tools says \"工具 writeRiskProfile 声明与命名空间拒绝（claw.a4.tool.choice on a1）由 tool-registration / trace-namespace-enforcement 覆盖\" — the coverage test should assert tool-registration enforcement (assertWriteRiskProfileForTool) and namespace rejection (validateEventNamespace('claw.a4.tool.choice','a1') → error). Good.\n\nLet me check the tool files' declarations."}}
