rite-risk profile to `openclaw_runtime/src/shared/tool-registration.ts`.\n2. Add `openclaw_runtime/src/agents/cross-area/cross-area-facades.ts`.\n3. Update the A1 spec requiredTests to include the counter tests.\n4. Add a01 coverage test + docs + sums.\n\nThat's a LOT and (2) especially is A2/A5/A6 territory. Hmm.\n\nWait. Let me re-check whether `cross-area-facades.ts` might already exist in the base but I checked the wrong path. Earlier in the agent tree: `Test-Path openclaw_runtime/src/agents/cross-area/cross-area-facades.ts` → False. And `Get-ChildItem openclaw_runtime/src/agents/cross-area` → nothing. Let me check if there's a `cross-area` dir at all, and search whole repo for \"cross-area-facades\".\n\nLet me also check whether maybe the reference `performance-budgets.test.ts` import is a *dynamic* optional import that fails only if the module is missing; the grader failure was a suite-level import error → hard fail.\n\nLet me search the base tree for cross-area.\n\nLet me run these checks now, then decide.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider the possibility that these two differing digests are NOT about the reference solution but about the *pristine upstream* repo at a later commit... no.\n\nLet me just look: maybe the task's `base` directory is itself \"base commit content\" and the oracle test files come from the grader directory = reference commit content. Let me check if the grader dir (baseline-1/grader) is the reference tree: it contains tool-registration.test.ts with durable_state (since the run failed on it). So grader tests = reference tests. And grader source? The grader dir would be the *agent's* source tree (after agent edits) — because the grader copies agent output. Let me check whether baseline-1/grader has the a1 spec with deprecation-counters (the agent's edit) and whether its openclaw_runtime has cross-area.\n\nLet me inspect baseline-1/grader to understand the grading tree composition. This is legitimate: it's the prior trial's artifact