n. And `baseCommit` in TASK.json = 72ba3c. Interesting: the workspace (agent dir) — is it at base or candidate? Let me check: workspace `harness/specs/tasks/a1-layered-isolation-pilot.md` = base version (`aa311d34`?). Let me verify. Earlier I read the workspace spec and it had requiredTests with 5 entries (no deprecation-counters) → consistent with base = candidate-without-reference-changes.\n\nHmm, but the candidate tree contains cross-area-facades.ts and durable_state. And the workspace does NOT. So the workspace ≠ candidate tree. Workspace = candidate tree MINUS the A01 reference changes. Let me verify that theory: workspace tool-registration.ts vs base vs candidate.\n\nLet me check workspace hashes for the key files. Actually I did read workspace tool-registration.ts and it had no durable_state, and no cross-area dir. So workspace = base-like.\n\nHmm, so what exactly is the workspace? It's an \"isolated repository task\" — `isolation: fresh git-archive directory per trial`. And the oracle test files were scrubbed. So workspace = some snapshot minus 6 test files.\n\nLet me just check the workspace against base and candidate for a broad file set. Let me do the efficient hash-object comparison.\n\nActually, most important: I need to know which version the 6 restored oracle tests will be. If they're the candidate versions, I need source changes. If they're the base versions, I don't.\n\nFrom the treatment-1 stderr, the restored `tool-registration.test.ts` DID test `durable_state` (5 failures). And the grader copy = candidate. So restored = candidate version. Confirmed empirically: `durable_state` tests ran and failed.\n\nAnd `performance-budgets.test.ts` failed to import cross-area-facades → the restored performance-budgets.test.ts is the candidate version (which imports it). Confirmed: grader copy = candidate.\n\nSo YES: I must port `durable_state` into tool-registration.ts and create cross-area-facades.ts, so the restored candidate-version oracle tests pass.\n\nHm