corresponds to the digest of the file *as the oracle expects it*, i.e. of the restored content. And the actually-restored content differs — no, `sourceInputsVerified: true`.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider. Maybe `4502f116` is the digest of the file *after* grader normalization (e.g., line-ending normalization or the version that includes some marker). Let me just check: compute sha256 of the grader file → 585be3c6. And the manifest digest → 4502f116. Let me look for a file with 4502f116 in the history.\n\nLet me search all blobs. Actually simpler: this could be the digest of the file in a *different* commit. Let me check `66...` Actually, let me check if there's another version.\n\nHmm — wait. Let me reconsider: maybe the manifest's inputDigests are the digests of the reference *solution* inputs. The `oracle.testPaths` are 7 files. The manifest lists 8 entries including the harness spec. Let me compute digests of all these from the candidate revision and compare:\n\n- harness/specs/tasks/harness-gap-a01-isolation-pilot-reverify.md: fabb4c13... matches candidate blob fabb4c13 ✓\n- tests/unit/tool-registration.test.ts: manifest 8690822b... matches candidate 8690822b ✓\n- policy-light-snapshotter: manifest c1d7fd82... matches ✓\n- shadow-write-event-recorder: manifest 8d86315c... matches ✓\n- trace-namespace-enforcement: manifest 8dfeebc6... matches ✓\n- performance-budgets: manifest 4502f116... candidate 585be3c6 ✗ MISMATCH\n- deprecation-counters: manifest bf5559f7... matches ✓\n- a01-a1-gate-coverage: manifest 23b311a2... matches ✓\n\nSo only performance-budgets differs. Interesting! So the manifest's expected digest for performance-budgets is `4502f116...`, but the candidate revision blob and the grader copy are `585be3c6...`.\n\nHmm, that means the grader restored a `performance-budgets.test.ts` whose digest is 585be3c6, but the manifest expects 4502f116. Yet `sourceInputsVerified: true` in the receipt. So maybe `inputDigests` is not about the restored content... Or maybe the verify only checks the agent-producible ones.\n\nHold on, actually — maybe I should check: what's the digest of performance-budgets.test.ts *in the grader directory of treatment-3*? We don't have a grader for treatment-3 yet (that's what I'm producing).\n\nLet me look at this differently. Actually, maybe `inputDigests` are digests of the *hidden* test inputs recorded at corpus-build time from a different revision (e.g., the reference solution revision at build time), which is not exactly the candidate commit. Let me find a blob with sha256 = 4502f116.\n\nLet me search.\n\nActually — this matters less than the main question. Let me focus. The key insight: since the grader verified `sourceInputsVerified: true` and the trial ran, the restored files are what matters. And the restored performance-budgets is `585be3c6` (candidate revision) which imports cross-area-facades. That's confirmed by the actual stderr failure: \"Failed to resolve import ... cross-area-facades.js\". \n\nSo my job is clear regardless. But the digest mismatch is worth noting — it suggests `inputDigests` may be digests of the oracle test files at *reference* time, and the grader tolerates them. Let me quickly find 4502f116 to be safe — it might reveal that the grader restores a *different* performance-budgets than candidate.\n\nLet me search git objects for that hash.\n\nActually, an easier approach: let me look at whether the trial-1 grader's performance-budgets.test.ts is identical to the candidate's. We computed: candidate blob 585be3c6 = grader file hash 585be3c6 → yes identical.\n\nSo we have 4502f116 as an unexplained digest. Let me try to find it. I'll scan all blobs of that path across all commits in D:\\ClawX.\n\nLet me do that.\n\nActually, I realize the important thing: my deliverable must make the ORACLE pass. The oracle restores those 6 files from... where? The grader tree at grading time. In trial-1 the grader tree had candidate-revision copies. For treatment-3, presumably the same. So I should target compatibility with the **candidate-revision** test files:\n- tool-registration.test.ts (candidate, needs durable_state)\n- performance-budgets.test.ts (candidate, needs cross-area-facades)\n- deprecation-counters.test.ts (same as base)\n- plus policy-light-snapshotter, shadow-write-event-recorder, trace-namespace-enforcement (same)\n\nAnd my own deliverable `tests/unit/a01-a1-gate-coverage.test.ts` must be the candidate version (23b311a2) — but wait, the grader keeps its own hidden copy of that. So whatever I write will be overwritten by the grader's hidden copy! Actually no — the manifest says `hiddenSignalPaths: [\"tests/unit/a01-a1-gate-coverage.test.ts\"]`. So the grader has its own copy and uses it. My copy just needs to... hmm.\n\nLet me think about this. In trial-1, `scrubbedOraclePaths` = the 6 preexisting tests. `a01-a1-gate-coverage.test.ts` was NOT in scrubbedOraclePaths — meaning it was NOT removed from the agent workspace. The agent was expected to CREATE it. And the grader had a hidden copy which it compared/used.\n\nHmm, but the oracle command runs `tests/unit/a01-a1-gate-coverage.test.ts` — from which tree? The oracle runs with the grader's tree merged. Since `hiddenSignalPaths` includes it, the grader presumably uses its own hidden version for the run (to prevent the agent from writing a trivial guard).\n\nActually looking at the trial-1 stderr: the a01-a1-gate-coverage test failure was in `tests/unit/a01-a1-gate-coverage.test.ts:17:18` — line 17 col 18: `expect(spec).toContain(\"tests/unit/deprecation-counters.test.ts\")`. The hidden copy I read from D:\\ClawX has that exact assertion at that line. So yes, the grader used its own hidden copy.\n\nSo for a01-a1-gate-coverage.test.ts, my deliverable will be replaced at grade time by the hidden copy (which is identical to the candidate version 23b311a2). So I should still create the file with content matching (23b311a2) to satisfy the deliverable + typecheck/lint, and it will be