cratch dir, junction/symlink node_modules from D:\\ClawX, restore the 6 oracle tests from grader, place my a01 coverage test, and run `pnpm exec vitest run ...`.\n\nThat's the cleanest reproduction. Let me do that: create `D:\\tmp\\rsi-tf0604-run5\\_a01verify\\` as a copy of my workspace + node_modules junction + grader tests.\n\nActually more efficient: create the scratch dir, use `robocopy` or `Copy-Item -Recurse` of the agent workspace (211 MB), then `New-Item -ItemType Junction` for node_modules pointing at D:\\ClawX\\node_modules.\n\nHmm, but `openclaw_runtime` has its own node_modules too and `packages/agent-core/node_modules`. In D:\\ClawX those exist. The workspace has no node_modules at all. So a junction for root node_modules + openclaw_runtime/node_modules + openclaw_runtime/packages/*/node_modules would be needed.\n\nSimpler: copy my workspace into `D:\\ClawX`-adjacent? No.\n\nAlternative: Run vitest from within `D:\\ClawX` but with `--root` pointed at my workspace? Vitest's root determines config resolution and test discovery. `--root` changes where config/include resolve. But module resolution for imports would still start from the importing file's location — which is my workspace. Node resolves node_modules by walking UP from the file. My workspace is at `D:\\tmp\\...\\agent` — no node_modules up the tree. So imports of `vitest`, `@testing-library/jest-dom` etc. would fail.\n\nSo I do need node_modules reachable. Cleanest: create a scratch copy of the workspace at a path where node_modules can be junctioned.\n\nLet me place scratch at `D:\\tmp\\_a01verify\\rsi`. Then junction `node_modules` -> `D:\\ClawX\\node_modules`, `openclaw_runtime\\node_modules` -> `D:\\ClawX\\openclaw_runtime\\node_modules`, `openclaw_runtime\\packages\\agent-core\\node_modules` -> corresponding.\n\nActually — does pnpm put packages' node_modules as real dirs inside each workspace package? Yes, pnpm creates `node_modules` in each workspace package with symlinks. `packages/agen