{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_522","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I need node_modules in the verify tree. Rather than copying (huge), I can use junctions. The pnpm node_modules has .pnpm store with hardlinks; junctions to D:\\ClawX\\node_modules should work for resolution, but nested package node_modules also needed (openclaw_runtime/node_modules, packages/*/node_modules).\n\nActually, simpler: create a junction `D:\\tmp\\rsi-tf0604-run5\\_a01verify\\ClawX\\node_modules` -> `D:\\ClawX\\node_modules`, and same for the nested ones. Vite's `fs.allow` uses `__dirname` of the config which is the verify tree, so /@fs requests for verify tree paths are allowed.\n\nBut the issue: node resolution of `vitest` etc. from the verify tree. With a junction, node will resolve `D:\\tmp\\...\\_a01verify\\ClawX\\node_modules\\vitest`. That works.\n\nHowever pnpm's node_modules is a symlink farm pointing into `.pnpm`. `.bin/vitest.cmd` references `%~dp0\\..\\.pnpm\\...`. If I junction the whole node_modules, the internal relative paths still work because they resolve inside D:\\ClawX\\node_modules\\.pnpm.\n\nHmm, but `NODE_PATH` in vitest.cmd points to D:\\ClawX paths — fine.\n\nActually, the cleanest approach: replicate what the grader did. The grader dir has `node_modules` as a Junction to `C:\\Users\\27933\\.codex\\worktrees\\tf0604-rsi-baseline\\ClawX\\node_modules`. And the nested node_modules dirs — let me check whether the grader has them.\n\nLet me check the grader for nested node_modules.\n\nActually wait — the grader has node_modules junction. Let me check if grader has openclaw_runtime/node_modules.\n\nLet me just do junctions for all the node_modules dirs that exist in D:\\ClawX.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. A simpler and more faithful test: I could copy the verify tree INTO D:\\ClawX's structure... no, that would modify the reference checkout.\n\nAlternative: I can create the verify tree as a directory next to D:\\ClawX, and create junctions for node_modules. Let me check whether vitest resolution works with just the top-level junction. The runtime imports like `../../openclaw_runtime/packages/agent-core/src/harness/compaction/compression-planner.js` are relative — they don't need node_modules. The `@openclaw/*` aliases are handled by the vite plugin. `openclaw/plugin-sdk/llm` type-only imports are erased.\n\nBut some imported runtime modules may import bare specifiers (e.g., 'typebox', '@earendil-works/pi-tui'). Vite resolves bare specifiers by walking up from the importer's directory to find node_modules. The importer is `verify/ClawX/openclaw_runtime/src/...`, so it walks up: `verify/ClawX/openclaw_runtime/node_modules` → if absent, `verify/ClawX/node_modules` → junction → found.\n\nSo I need at minimum the top-level junction, plus `openclaw_runtime/node_modules` (pnpm workspace package deps) and the nested package node_modules.\n\nLet me create junctions for each.\n\nActually simpler: create junctions for the specific ones:\n- node_modules\n- openclaw_runtime/node_modules\n- openclaw_runtime/packages/*/node_modules\n\nLet me enumerate all node_modules dirs at depth <= 3 in D:\\ClawX (source), excluding build/ and .pnpm internal ones, and create matching junctions in the verify tree.\n\nThe list from before (D:\\ClawX):\n- D:\\ClawX\\node_modules\n- D:\\ClawX\\.tmp\\agent-acceptance-fixture-BKbWHv\\node_modules (skip)\n- D:\\ClawX\\build\\... (skip)\n- D:\\ClawX\\openclaw_runtime\\node_modules\n- D:\\ClawX\\openclaw_runtime\\packages\\{acp-core,agent-core,gateway-client,gateway-protocol,llm-core,llm-runtime,markdown-core,media-core,net-policy,sdk,speech-core,terminal-core}\\node_modules\n- D:\\ClawX\\openclaw_runtime\\ui\\node_modules\n- D:\\ClawX\\packages\\versebrowser\\node_modules\n- D:\\ClawX\\resources\\verseshell-mcp\\node_modules\n\nLet me create those junctions in the verify tree.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider whether this local verification is worth the effort vs. risk. The task says \"run rele